<section>
<h2>Virtual Dip Simulator</h2>
    
    <p>To allow experiments with various objects and configurable parameters, and to prove that the concept can provide reasonable results, it makes sense to start with 
    a virtual dip machine. The virtual dipping machine takes in various parameters and outputs a tensor of example output that one would see after using a dip machine, 
    generating regular measurements of the amount of "water" displaced, for vairous orientations (phi, theta).</p>

        <img src="/images/submerged.png">

        <h3> 1. Voxelization</h3>
        <p>The class age old story of moving from a continuous domain to a discrete one arises again, with the usual problems of crude approximations.</p>
        <p>

            We divide the mesh into a cubic voxelized grid using the "standard voxelization method". 
            The longest mesh side is taken to determine the voxel pitch(mm) needed to create a arbitrary 100x100x100 voxel grid. A more sophisticated approach
            would be to estimate the grid resolution by comparing the percent error of the actual versus voxelized volume, and picking a threshold of allowed error. 
            Perhaps a later update for version 2.

        </p>
        <img src="/images/voxel_sphere.png">

        <h3> 2. Generate Orientations</h3>
        <p>
        Given an infinite number of orientations (i.e inifinte dips), we would theoretically able to solve the shape perfectly.As (rather unfortunatley) we are limited to building a discrete dipping machine, we must satisfy ourselves with the practicle application 
        of making an estimation with a limited number of dipping orientations. 
        </p>
        
        
        <p>
        The user inputs the number of dips k that they would like to simulate, and from this we find the corresponding k number of orientations (phi, theta) in a uniform distribution.
        The original research did not provide how these orientations where calculated, therfore the decision was made to use the Fibonnaci Sphere. Other options could be the equi angular gird.
        </p>

        <h4>Fibonacci Sphere</h4>
        <p>An algorithm that defines the distribution of k points approximately uniformely across a sphere using a golden-angle spiral.</p>

        <img src="/images/fibonacci.png">
            
            golden_angle = pi(3 - root(5))
            
            Algorithm: 
                For in i (0, N-1):
                    calculate (x,y,z) by proejctign the spiral onto spherical coordinates
                    z = 1- (i/(N-1))
                    golden_angle = np.pi * (3 - np.sqrt(5)) 
                    phi = i x golden-angle
                    theta = arccos(z)
                    x = rcos(theta)
                    y = rsin(theta)


        <h3> 3. Virtual dipping </h3>
        For each orientation, we proceed to "dip" the object by dividing it in equally spaced slices, and counting the number of voxels in each slice, to define a single dip measure. 
        
        Rotate the dipping direction, move virtual water plane through the object and count the number of voxels submerged.

        As we rotate the object (i.e iterate thought the orientations) we calculate the direction of the center of the object normal to the direction of the water. 
        We then project each coordinate (specifically each voxel center) onto the normal vector (normal to the water surface).
        We divide the total water height into m uniform slices (in this simulation m is fixed to 100, but this could be made a configurable parameter).
        We then easy count the number of voxel centers that are submerged in each water slice, by comparing if the 
        voxel center is less than or equal to cumulated water slice height. 
        That is, 

        <p>V(h) = sum_{i=1}^N *1* (p_i * n <= h)  </p>
        <ul>
            <li>p_i: position of the voxel centers </li>
            <li>h: height of the water slice</li>
            <li>n: normal unit vector orthogonal to the water surface (for the given dipping orientation)</li>
       </ul>

       <h4>4. File Ouput</h4>
       <p>
            The dip sumulator generates a .csv file with the layout: 
                    
            columns = ["dip no.", "theta", "phi", "water_height_measurement_mm", "displace_vol_mm3"]

            This is also the expected input format for the 3d shape reconstructor.
       </p>

