<section>
    <h2>3D Shape Reconstruction</h2>
        <p>Now to the exciting parts: how can we possibly create a 3 dimensional object from 1 dimensional information? </p>
        <p>The solution is the same for all data related problems: more data and linear algebra.</p>

        <h3>1. Voxelization</h3>
        <p>Before exploring the dip data, we first need to define the space in which we will build the voxelized object.</p>    

            Using the predetermined resolution (future parameter?) of a 100x100x100 cubic voxelization, centered at the origin, we estimate the 
             size of the voxels by using the maximum water height in the dataset, with some variable padding for breathing room.
        
        <ul>
        <b>Useful vocabulary:</b>
            <li>extent: length (mm) of one axis of the voxel grid</li>
            <li>pitch: length (mm) of one cubic voxel</li>
            <li>resolution: number of voxels of one grid </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>2. Measurement resampling</h3>

        <p>
        The model assumes uniformely spaced water height slices in each dip. 
        Although this is the case from the output of the virtual simulator, this would not occur in practicle application. Therefore we need to interpolate the cumulative measurements to
        estimate m uniform samples. 
        </p>

        <p>
        This is due to as you lower the object at a constant height step, the corresponding water measure will be a constant plus the amount of object submerged.
        The change in water level is not expected to be constant if the volume of the object is not distrubuted equally.
        </p>

        <p>Note: The number of uniform slices is bound by the water elavation rate.</p>

        <b>Piecewise Cubic Hermite Interpolating Polynomial (PCHIP) Interpolation</b>
        <p>The original authors of the algorithm did not specify a Interpolation method, thus with freedom of choice, thus we take PCHIP Interpolation.
            The algorithm preserves monotonicity which is a valid assumption to take advantage of in our dipping, and can handle data that is not smooth, 
            ideally being equiped to handle potential measurement and calibration errors.
        </p>

        <img src="/images/resampling.png">


        <h3>3. Inverse optimization setup</h3>
        <p>Given our dip measurements we now define a inverse optimization problem to solve for the object that could 
            construct such dip patterns. We want to solve for a voxelized object f that over various orientations and 
            dipping layers (expressed in B) yields the vectorized dip measurements g.
        </p>
        <p>arg min f  ||g - Bf||</p>


        <h4>B "rotate and sum" matrix </h4>
        <p>But how to design such a system that captures the various orientations and slicing combinations?</p>
        <p>To build the matrix B, we break it into two operations: rotation and summation. That is B can be expressed as: </p>

        <p>B = S * R </p>

        <p><b>Rotation matrix R:</b> a sparse vector of rotation matrices, storing the interpolated rotated voxel center positions, for each orientation (phi, theta)
            </p>
            
        <p>
            R = ( R_(phi, theta)_1  R_(phi, theta)_2  ...  R_(phi, theta)_k ) /in R ^ (kN x N) 
        </p>

        <p>Note the size: in our N=100x100x100 resolution grid, the size of R is the number of dips (ideally ranging from 400-1000+) x 1,000,000**2, with 8 nonzero entries per row on average.</p>
        

        <ul>
            <b>Constructing R: </b>
            <li>
                    For each orientation, we construct a rotational basis, that acts as a orthonormal 
                    coordinate system that is aligned with our unit normal vector (normal to the surface of the water). This is typical 3x3 rotation matrix, where 
                    z is the unit normal, and x and y are unit vectors orthogonal to z and each other.  
            </li>
            <li>
                    Using the rotational basis, we compute the rotation operations R_(phi, theta) by projecting each voxel center positions onto the rotational basis, to 
                    get the rotated voxel positions. But, there is an issue: the updated voxel positions do not necessarily map to a new voxel but rather offcenter. To solve this, 
                    we use trilinear interpolation to check the 8 neighboring voxels. The interpolation weights are stored per row. Meaning each row contains at most 8 non-zero entries.
            </li>
            <li>    Stacking the rotation operators together for each orientation builds a complete rotation matrix.
            </li>
        </ul>

    
        //TODO create image (simple example?) explaning the inteprolation number 8, showing it is necessary as the new voxel center posisiton does not necessarily map 
    to a new center, but rather in between 8 



        <p><b>Summation matrix S: </b> a binary diagonal matrix of matrices, that gives the number of "active" voxels in each complete slice, for each dip
            S = diag(S_bar, S_bar, ... S_bar)
        </p>

        <p>Note the size: S^bar in R ^(MxN) and S in R^(kMxKM) , with k number of dips, M number of slices and N = 1,000,0000</p>
        


        <ul>
            <b>Constructing S: </b>
            <li>
                In practice computing S is done with the kronecker product of a identity matrix and a column vector of 1s 


            </li>

            <li>
                def summation_matrix(n, n_dips):
                    #one slice is the sum of n*n voxels
                    S_bar = kron(csr_matrix(np.ones((1, n*n))), 
                            eye(n, format="csr"), 
                            format="csr")

                    S = block_diag ([S_bar] * n_dips, format = "csr")
                    return S
            </li>
        </ul>


